on/math-academy-id "q-124455" :question/problem "What is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{140}^{∘}$ expressed in terms of a reference angle?" :question/worked-solution "To express $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{140}^{∘}$ in terms of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\nFirst, let's draw the angle ${140}^{∘}$ in the coordinate plane (CAST diagram):\n\n![](images/5f/5f2b641a3a765f65f1d7d11837193341edb6876dfaea45e9eeaf1d7610178435.png)\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta ={140}^{∘}$ is in the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & ={180}^{∘}-\\theta  \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{140}^{∘} \\\\  & ={40}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{140}^{∘}$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{40}^{∘}.$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{140}^{∘}$ must be *negative* because the cosine ratio is *always* negative in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡{140}^{∘}=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡{40}^{∘}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-290ee7a2-24f2-5c09-8d37-e125475616d5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-290ee7a2-24f2-5c09-8d37-e125475616d5" :answer-field/id #uuid "290ee7a2-24f2-5c09-8d37-e125475616d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5b696c45-4f0f-51d6-a0a5-7c025c4830aa" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5b696c45-4f0f-51d6-a0a5-7c025c4830aa" "answer-349f69bc-bcdc-5122-ac2c-a188f806b7b1" "answer-3f8a5f81-69f6-532a-bfcc-6895c9a965a1" "answer-2a2505a0-14df-51c6-ad4c-3b9e3be1a49c" "answer-5722dc1e-7879-5efa-9e3e-63d11d333004"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b69